  <!-- ── Section 2 ────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Moving to a traditional bank</h2>

  <p>
    Once you have a proof of address — a tenancy agreement, utility bill, or council tax letter —
    you can open an account with a high street bank. This is worth doing eventually for a few reasons:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Traditional banks offer mortgages, overdrafts and savings accounts once you're established</li>
    <li>In-branch support for more complex queries</li>
    <li>Some older employers' payroll systems occasionally have issues processing digital bank sort codes (rare, but it does happen)</li>
    <li>Credit building — a traditional bank relationship helps establish your UK credit history over time</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What you'll typically need</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Passport or national ID</li>
    <li>Proof of UK address — a utility bill, bank statement, tenancy agreement or council tax letter dated within the last 3 months</li>
    <li>Sometimes: a reference letter from an employer or university</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The main high street banks are <strong>Barclays, HSBC, Lloyds, NatWest</strong> and
    <strong>Santander</strong>. Branch staff vary in how helpful they are for new arrivals —
    if one branch turns you away, try another.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">Worth knowing</span>
    <p>
      <strong>HSBC</strong> has a dedicated international banking service that can sometimes be
      opened before you arrive in the UK — useful if you already hold an HSBC account in your
      home country. Check <a href="https://www.hsbc.co.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">hsbc.co.uk</a> for current eligibility.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Section 3 ────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>International money transfers</h2>

  <p>
    If you're sending money home, receiving transfers from family, or managing finances across
    multiple countries, high street banks tend to charge high fees and offer poor exchange rates.
    <strong>Wise</strong> (formerly TransferWise) is widely considered the best option for
    international transfers — it uses the real mid-market exchange rate and charges transparent,
    low fees.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can also set up a Wise account with UK banking details <em>before you arrive</em>,
    which is useful if your employer needs a UK account number quickly.
  </p>

