  <!-- ── Section 4 ────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>What about Revolut?</h2>

  <p>
    Revolut is a popular app for travel and managing multiple currencies, and many new arrivals
    use it. However, it's important to understand what it is and isn't:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>In the UK, Revolut operates as an <strong>e-money institution</strong>, not a bank</li>
    <li>This means your money is <strong>not covered by the FSCS</strong> (the £85,000 guarantee that protects deposits at Monzo, Starling and high street banks)</li>
    <li>Revolut is useful as a secondary card for travel or currency exchange — but it shouldn't be your primary UK account</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout callout--note">
    <span class="callout__heading">Check the latest</span>
    <p>
      Revolut has been pursuing a full UK banking licence for some time. Their regulatory status
      may have changed — check <a href="https://www.revolut.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">revolut.com</a> for their current position before making decisions.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Section 5 ────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Building your UK credit score</h2>

  <p>
    Your UK credit history starts at zero when you arrive — regardless of your financial history
    elsewhere. This affects your ability to get phone contracts, rent a flat without a guarantor,
    and eventually get a mortgage. It takes time to build, but there are a few things that help:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Register on the electoral roll</strong> — even as a non-UK citizen if you're from an EU/EEA country. It's one of the simplest ways to improve your credit score.</li>
    <li><strong>Get a credit builder card</strong> — both Monzo and Starling offer features to help. There are also specialist cards (like Aqua or Capital One) designed for people with limited UK credit history.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay everything on time</strong> — utility bills, subscriptions, any direct debits. Consistency matters.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep your oldest accounts open</strong> — length of credit history counts.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The three UK credit agencies are <strong>Experian, Equifax</strong> and
    <strong>TransUnion</strong>. You can check your report for free via
    <a href="https://www.checkmyfile.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CheckMyFile</a>
    (which shows all three), or individually through each agency's free tier.
  </p>

  <!-- ── Section 6 ────────────────────────── -->
  <h2>Quick reference: things to know</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>FSCS protection</strong> covers up to £85,000 per person per institution. Monzo, Starling and all high street banks are covered. Revolut is not (as of publication).</li>
    <li><strong>Save your bank statements</strong> from day one — you'll need 3–6 months of statements for flat applications, and sometimes for visa renewals.</li>
    <li><strong>SIM before bank</strong> — most digital banks require a UK phone number, so buying a SIM is literally step one.</li>
    <li><strong>Joint accounts</strong> — available at most banks once you've been a customer for a period. Ask your bank for their specific requirements.</li>
    <li><strong>Student accounts</strong> — if you're arriving as a student, Nationwide, NatWest and HSBC all offer competitive student current accounts with interest-free overdrafts.</li>
  </ul>

